<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Chutia kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1187 CE – 1524 CE (approx.) · capital Sadiya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom of the upper Brahmaputra valley centred on Sadiya, the main rival of the Ahom kingdom until its conquest in 1524.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Chutia kingdom took shape in the far east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadiya">Sadiya</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arunachal-pradesh">Arunachal Pradesh</a>. Its traditional founding is dated to 1187 under a ruler remembered as Birpal, though the early history rests mainly on later chronicles. By the 14th century it was a significant power north of the river.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chutia-people">Chutias</a> were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodo-kachari-peoples">Bodo-Kachari</a> people whose rulers adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> forms of kingship, patronising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shakta</a> worship, notably the Tamreswari temple near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadiya">Sadiya</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a>-plate inscriptions of the 14th and 15th centuries record land grants and use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and early Assamese. The kingdom was known for metalwork, including the manufacture of guns and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a>, and for its control of trade routes to the hills.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After a long series of wars, the Ahom king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suhungmung">Suhungmung</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chutia-people">Chutias</a> and annexed their kingdom in 1523 to 1524. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadiya">Sadiya</a> became the seat of an Ahom frontier governor, the Sadiya-khowa Gohain, and many Chutia nobles and artisans were absorbed into Ahom society.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chutia-kingdom.jpg" alt="Chutia kingdom" width="320" height="206"><figcaption>Image: Ananya Taye · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stone_inscription_of_Chutia_king_Muktadharmanarayana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1187 CE – 1524 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sadiya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dhirnarayan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism (Shaktism)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Assamese, Deori</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chutiya kingdom, Sadiya kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1356&amp;focus=chutia-kingdom" aria-label="Chutia kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7189 1158 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chutia kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7509 1414L7494 1445"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7494 1445h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7509 1414h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7509 1414h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sadiya. <a href="/atlas?year=1356&amp;focus=chutia-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
